Z||000000||84fierotrevor||12-15-2001||11:31 PM||||I got a new oil filter and air filter and my engine just had the new head put on, But my fiero keep's dying ! it just turns off<BR>it normaly happens when I am going at a low speed . I sorta just loose accelortation<BR>then it dies!<BR>any one know what it may be ?||||reg||9
Z||000001||ITB 2M4||12-15-2001||11:49 PM||||It could be any number of things, unfortunately.  A bad fuel pump, clogged fuel filter, or even something like your distributor being out on the timing.<P>Does it die out when just sitting, or does it happen under load?<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||4
Z||000002||Mach10||12-15-2001||11:54 PM||||Ignition, or fuel delivery... Bad Fuel pump relay... Bad oil sender... Bad coil/Ignition control module/pickup coil||||reg||9
Z||000003||84fierotrevor||12-16-2001||12:33 AM||||it dies when sitting or sometimes when trying to accleorate slowly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTDude||12-16-2001||11:25 AM||||Major question.  Does it start right back up?  This is key to diagnosis.  Does it jump and jerk as it comes to a stop? Automatic or 4 spd?<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT<p>[This message has been edited by GTDude (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||camon||12-16-2001||02:34 PM||||Sorry to say but that sounds like a fuel pump (you may get lucky and it maybe the pump relay...don't count on it though). I hate to say it because I still have that bad taste left in my mouth from my fuel pump change (not a fun job) but it sure sounds like the pump is going out. You should be able to check the fuel pump using a fuel pressure gauge to check your fuel pressure!<BR> <BR>I had simular problems. Major hesitation (when applying throttle and cruising at low speeds), car seemed to want to stall at times when stopping. Well then one day rolls around...i'm driving and the car had no throttle response when I pulled away from a redlight. It then started bucking and carrying on. Luckily I was right up the street from my house and I got the car to the house when it stalled completely!<P>I hope it is something else in your case because I had one heck of a time replacing mine (1/2 a tank of fuel in it) not to mention the time I had getting those hoses off the tank fittings and back on. If you run the car stay close to home and run the car with less then 1/2 a tank of fuel at all times in case it is the fuel pump (then when you drop the tank to replace the pump you don't have all that fuel in the tank)!<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
